From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mikhail Malyshev <mike.malyshev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 23:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ui/dbus: stop listening when the peer disconnects

A DBusDisplayListener unregisters itself from the display only in
dispose(), and dispose never runs when a peer goes away.

dbus_display_listener_new() installs a connection filter whose user data
is a reference to the listener, and the listener keeps a reference to the
connection. That is a cycle: when the remote peer vanishes neither object
is finalised, so the DisplayChangeListener stays on the display's list for
the life of the machine. Every console update is then formatted and
dispatched to a peer that is not there, and the sends fail one after
another.

The cost is per client that has ever attached, so it accumulates. Found on
an edge device whose graphical console had been restarted a number of
times: seven dbus-gl listeners on one console, six of them dead. That also
multiplied the QEMUCursor reference traffic by seven, which is what turned
a narrow refcount race into a reproducible double free.

Unregister from the connection's "closed" signal instead, guarded by
dcl.ds so that a later dispose does not remove an already-removed node.

This does not break the reference cycle itself - the listener object and
the connection still leak their memory - but it stops the dead listener
being called, which is the part that costs anything at runtime. Breaking
the cycle properly means dropping the filter from the closed handler, and
that wants more care about the listener being finalised inside its own
callback than seemed wise to combine with this.

Note "closed" is emitted in the thread-default main context of the
connection, which for these connections is the main loop, so the
unregistration happens on the main thread as the display list requires.

Signed-off-by: Mikhail Malyshev <mike.malyshev@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/tools/qemu-xen/ui/dbus-listener.c b/tools/qemu-xen/ui/dbus-listener.c
index a54123acea..c93f9ff990 100644
--- a/tools/qemu-xen/ui/dbus-listener.c
+++ b/tools/qemu-xen/ui/dbus-listener.c
@@ -793,12 +793,45 @@ const DisplayChangeListenerOps dbus_dcl_ops = {
     .dpy_cursor_define       = dbus_cursor_define,
 };
 
+/*
+ * The peer went away. Stop being a display listener now.
+ *
+ * Waiting for dispose is not enough: the connection filter installed in
+ * dbus_display_listener_new() holds a reference to us, and we hold one to
+ * the connection, so neither object is finalised when the peer vanishes and
+ * dispose may never run at all. The listener then stays on the display's
+ * list for the lifetime of the machine, and every console update is
+ * delivered to a dead peer - a cost that accumulates with every client that
+ * has ever attached.
+ *
+ * Observed on an edge device whose graphical console had reconnected a
+ * number of times: seven listeners on one console, six of them dead, each
+ * one multiplying the per-update work and the cursor refcount traffic.
+ */
+static void
+dbus_display_listener_closed(GDBusConnection *conn G_GNUC_UNUSED,
+                             gboolean remote_peer_vanished G_GNUC_UNUSED,
+                             GError *error G_GNUC_UNUSED,
+                             gpointer user_data)
+{
+    DBusDisplayListener *ddl = DBUS_DISPLAY_LISTENER(user_data);
+
+    /* dcl.ds is set by register_displaychangelistener and cleared by
+     * unregister_displaychangelistener, so it says whether we are still on
+     * the list. Unregistering twice would remove an already-removed node. */
+    if (ddl->dcl.ds) {
+        unregister_displaychangelistener(&ddl->dcl);
+    }
+}
+
 static void
 dbus_display_listener_dispose(GObject *object)
 {
     DBusDisplayListener *ddl = DBUS_DISPLAY_LISTENER(object);
 
-    unregister_displaychangelistener(&ddl->dcl);
+    if (ddl->dcl.ds) {
+        unregister_displaychangelistener(&ddl->dcl);
+    }
     g_clear_object(&ddl->conn);
     g_clear_pointer(&ddl->bus_name, g_free);
     g_clear_object(&ddl->proxy);
@@ -1036,6 +1069,12 @@ dbus_display_listener_new(const char *bus_name,
     ddl->conn = conn;
     ddl->console = console;
 
+    /* _object so the handler goes away with us, rather than being left
+     * pointing at a freed listener if the connection outlives it. */
+    g_signal_connect_object(conn, "closed",
+                            G_CALLBACK(dbus_display_listener_closed),
+                            ddl, 0);
+
     dbus_display_listener_setup_shared_map(ddl);
     dbus_display_listener_setup_d3d11(ddl);
 
